    <div class="sun"></div>
    <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```
This code creates a simple div element that will serve as the sun image. The `.sun` class defines its dimensions and styling, including its circular shape and a white background color. The `@keyframes` rule defines an animation called "shine" that rotates the sun around in a circle over a period of three seconds.
Next, we need to create an `app.js` file that will handle the logic for changing the appearance of the sun based on the current time of day. Here's some sample code for doing this:
```
const currentDate = new Date();
const hours = currentDate.getHours();
if (hours >= 0 && hours <= 4) {
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.add('night');
} else if (hours > 4 && hours <= 12) {
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.remove('night');
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.add('day');
} else if (hours > 12 && hours <= 16) {
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.remove('day');
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.add('night');
} else if (hours > 16 && hours <= 20) {
    document.querySelector('.sun').classList.remove('night');
}
```
This code uses JavaScript to get the current hour of the day and then applies different classes to the sun element depending on whether it's currently daytime or nighttime. If it's between 4 AM and 12 PM, the sun is shown in a bright yellow circle. If it's after noon until sunset, the sun is shown as a red and orange swirl. And finally, if it's after sunset until midnight, the sun disappears completely.
